# Line-ending policy.
#
# ## Why this file exists
#
# This repository had `core.autocrlf=true` and no `.gitattributes`, so a checkout
# materialized CRLF while git stored LF. That is invisible until something hashes
# file bytes — and `docs/MANIFEST.json` does exactly that. The result was a
# manifest that verified on the machine that generated it and mismatched
# everywhere else, including against the very blobs `git show` returns for the
# commit containing it.
#
# Two independent fixes, because either alone leaves a hole:
#
#   1. the manifest generator hashes LF-NORMALIZED bytes, so the hash is a
#      property of the content rather than of the checkout;
#   2. this file pins the checkout to LF for text, so a working tree cannot
#      disagree with the blob in the first place.
#
# Both are present on purpose: (1) keeps an existing manifest meaningful, (2)
# stops new divergence. A tool that bypasses one is still covered by the other.

# Normalize every text file to LF in the repository, and check out LF too.
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text; `eol=lf` fixes the working tree.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets must never be normalized: rewriting their bytes corrupts them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip binary
*.tgz binary

# Windows-only launchers need CRLF to run correctly, and are not hashed.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts must stay LF or their shebang breaks under a POSIX shell.
*.sh text eol=lf

# `pnpm-lock.yaml` and other generated lockfiles are compared byte-wise by their
# tooling; keep them exactly as published.
pnpm-lock.yaml -text
package-lock.json -text
